<h1>Video 039 — native, static, transient</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Core Java With OCJP/SCJP: Declarations and Access Modifiers Part-10|| native,static,transient</p>
<p>ASR decode notes (Whisper): CRC++ / NANJava / non-java → C / C++ / non-Java; mission → machine; I 3.754 / I-5754 / I3bj754 → IEEE 754; stricty of a p / Stricty-FFP / strict FAP → strictfp; Hasch / Hascode → hashCode; Javiyam / Chaviyam / Jeeviya → JVM; Transient / wallet tile keyboard → transient / volatile keyword; De-sabilization → deserialization; synced you / secure consign → security constraint; Intra-faces → interfaces; E-N-M → enum; bhalks / bloggs → blocks; Wired / why it → void; stringer A → String[] args; should a code / sudo code → pseudo code; N switha → ends with; Floor form → platform; Anishka / Anushka → sample password; abc.S / abc.ser → .ser file; threats → threads; GIFRI → every; nathima → nothing but; Kampal / compulsive → compulsory; J N I → JNI.</p>
<p>What this lecture covers (actual board order): native (methods only) → transient (variables / serialization) → volatile (variables / threads; almost deprecated in his framing) → big 12-modifier applicability summary table → exam-style “only for …” conclusions. (static was Video 038; title lists it, but this Part-10 board is native / transient / volatile + summary.)</p>
<h2>Detailed notes</h2>
<h3>00:04 — Agenda: next modifier is `native`</h3>
<p>After earlier modifiers, next board topic: `native`.</p>
<h3>00:28 — Where can we apply `native`?</h3>
<p>First point (repeat until clear):</p>
<ul><li>native is a modifier.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Applicable only for methods.</li></ul>
<ul><li>We cannot apply native anywhere else (not on classes, variables, blocks, etc.).</li></ul>
<ul><li>It is a method-specific modifier.</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>// CE: modifier native not allowed here (class)
native class Test {
}
class Test {
    // CE: modifier native not allowed here (variable)
    native int x = 10;
}
class Test {
    // valid place: method declaration
    public native void m1();
}</pre>
<p>Board conclusion:</p>
<p>native is a modifier applicable only for methods and we can&#x27;t apply anywhere else.</p>
<h3>01:55 — What is a native method?</h3>
<p>Not “methods from our native place.”</p>
<p>Native methods = methods whose implementation is in a non-Java language (mostly C / C++).</p>
<p>Also called foreign methods — functionality coming from another language.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Demo {
    // declaration in Java; body lives in native (C/C++) code
    public native int compute();
}</pre>
<h3>03:30 — Why depend on C/C++ if Java has so many “stars”?</h3>
<p>Joke / motivation: Java is called simple, robust, secure, OO, multithreaded, platform independent, architecture neutral, … — still we use native for old C/C++. Why?</p>
<p>There are areas where Java is not up to the mark. To fill those gaps → native.</p>
<h3>06:48 — Objective 1: improve performance</h3>
<p>Main important gap: performance.</p>
<p>Wherever performance is very critical, Java is comparatively weaker than C/C++. As a Java programmer, don’t casually claim “Java is fastest.”</p>
<p>Pattern on board:</p>
<ol><li>Design most of the app in Java.</li></ol>
<ol><li>For the performance-critical slice → implement in C/C++.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Bring that functionality into Java with the `native` keyword.</li></ol>
<p>Biggest / first objective of `native`: improve performance of the system.</p>
<h3>09:05 — Objective 2: machine-level / memory-level communication</h3>
<p>Second problem with Java:</p>
<ul><li>Java is programmer-friendly, not machine-friendly.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Java cannot talk directly to the machine / memory the way C can.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Device drivers / OS-level work → typically C, not Java.</li></ul>
<p>Wherever machine-level or memory-level communication is required → implement that part in C/C++, expose it to Java via `native`.</p>
<p>Best example: `hashCode()`</p>
<ul><li>Hash code is generated based on address.</li></ul>
<ul><li>In pure Java there is no way to identify the address of an object.</li></ul>
<ul><li>That part is implemented in C/C++ and made available as a native method.</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>public class Object {
    // conceptual signature as on board (Object API)
    public native int hashCode();
}</pre>
<p>So: performance (objective 1) + mission/memory-level communication (objective 2).</p>
<h3>11:26 — Objective 3: reuse legacy non-Java code</h3>
<p>Third reason:</p>
<ul><li>You want some XYZ functionality.</li></ul>
<ul><li>It already exists in C/C++ legacy libraries.</li></ul>
<ul><li>You are not interested in rewriting it in Java.</li></ul>
<p>Then use `native` to call already existing legacy non-Java code.</p>
<h3>12:20 — Board: main objectives of `native` keyword</h3>
<p>Even with several benefits, he stresses the one most important practical benefit: fill Java’s performance gap with C/C++ via native.</p>
<h3>14:42 — Pseudo code to use `native` in Java (3 steps)</h3>
<p>Pseudo code / steps:</p>
<ol><li>Load native libraries</li></ol>
<ol><li>Declare native method</li></ol>
<ol><li>Invoke native method</li></ol>
<h4>Step 1 — load native libraries (in a `static` block)</h4>
<p>At class loading time, corresponding native libraries must be loaded.</p>
<p>Static blocks run at class loading → put System.loadLibrary(...) there.</p>
<pre class='code'>class NativeDemo {
    static {
        // load native libraries at class-loading time
        System.loadLibrary(&quot;nativeLibraryPath&quot;);
        // or: System.load(&quot;/absolute/path/to/lib.so&quot;);
    }
}</pre>
<h4>Step 2 — declare native method (ends with `;`)</h4>
<p>Implementation already exists in C/C++ — you are not responsible to provide a Java body.</p>
<p>Same idea as abstract method declarations: end with semicolon, not { }.</p>
<pre class='code'>class NativeDemo {
    static {
        System.loadLibrary(&quot;nativeLibraryPath&quot;);
    }

    // declare native method — ends with semicolon
    public native void m1();
}</pre>
<h4>Step 3 — invoke native method (from client / `main`)</h4>
<p>Instance method → create object, then call.</p>
<pre class='code'>class NativeDemo {
    static {
        System.loadLibrary(&quot;nativeLibraryPath&quot;);
    }

    public native void m1();
}

class Client {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        NativeDemo n = new NativeDemo();
        n.m1(); // invoke native method
    }
}</pre>
<p>JNI note (high level): Internally JNI (Java Native Interface) / DLL / mapping layers are involved when calling into C/C++. As application programmers for this OCJP chapter, remember the three steps; deep JNI coding is not the focus here.</p>
<p>Board recap of steps:</p>
<ol><li>Load native libraries</li></ol>
<ol><li>Declare native method</li></ol>
<ol><li>Invoke native method</li></ol>
<h3>21:48 — Native methods cannot have a body</h3>
<p>Because implementation is already available in C/C++:</p>
<ul><li>Declaration must end with `;`</li></ul>
<ul><li>Opening { } body → compile-time error: native methods cannot have a body</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    // valid
    public native void m1();
}
class Test {
    // invalid — native methods cannot have a body
    public native void m1() {
    }
    // CE: native methods cannot have a body
}</pre>
<p>Side-by-side on board:</p>
<h3>25:10 — Illegal combo: `abstract` + `native`</h3>
<p>Contradiction → `native abstract` is an illegal combination for methods.</p>
<pre class='code'>abstract class Test {
    // CE: illegal combination of modifiers: abstract and native
    public abstract native void m1();
}
abstract class Test {
    // CE: illegal combination of modifiers: native and abstract
    public native abstract void m1();
}</pre>
<p>Board line:</p>
<p>Hence we can&#x27;t declare native method as abstract.</p>
<p>native abstract combination is illegal combination for methods.</p>
<h3>27:54 — Illegal combo: `native` + `strictfp`</h3>
<p>Recall strictfp (from earlier class):</p>
<ul><li>If a method is strictfp, all floating-point calculations in that method must follow IEEE 754.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Goal: platform-independent floating-point results.</li></ul>
<ul><li>IEEE 754 / strictfp is a Java-language story.</li></ul>
<p>But a native method’s body is in C/C++:</p>
<ul><li>No guarantee that old languages follow IEEE 754.</li></ul>
<ul><li>C/C++ are platform-dependent.</li></ul>
<p>Therefore: cannot declare a native method as `strictfp`.</p>
<p>`native strictfp` is an illegal combination for methods.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    // CE: illegal combination of modifiers: native and strictfp
    public native strictfp void m1();
}
class Test {
    // CE: illegal combination of modifiers: strictfp and native
    public strictfp native void calculate();
}</pre>
<p>Board:</p>
<p>We can&#x27;t declare native method as strictfp because there is no guarantee that old languages follow IEEE 754 standard.</p>
<p>Hence native strictfp combination is illegal combination for methods.</p>
<p>Also remember: `native` + `abstract` illegal, `native` + `strictfp` illegal.</p>
<h3>31:16 — Advantage vs disadvantage of `native`</h3>
<p>Main advantage: performance of the application improves (critical parts in C/C++).</p>
<p>Main disadvantage: breaks platform-independent nature of Java.</p>
<p>Reason: you depend on C/C++, which are platform-dependent → your Java program can become platform-dependent too.</p>
<p>Example story with hashCode():</p>
<ul><li>On one system, hashCode() may produce one number.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Change the system → may get another number.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Because the method is not pure Java.</li></ul>
<p>Wherever you use native, don’t always expect platform-independent results.</p>
<pre class='code'>class HashDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Object o = new Object();
        System.out.println(o.hashCode());
        // number depends on JVM / native implementation / run —
        // may differ across platforms / runs
    }
}</pre>
<p>Board summary for native:</p>
<h3>35:10 — Next keyword: `transient`</h3>
<p>After finishing native, board moves to `transient`.</p>
<h3>35:40 — Where can we apply `transient`?</h3>
<p>Point 1:</p>
<ul><li>transient is a modifier.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Applicable only for variables.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Variable-specific modifier — cannot apply anywhere else.</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    // CE: modifier transient not allowed here
    transient void m1() {
    }
}
class Account {
    // valid: variable
    transient String password;
}</pre>
<p>Point 2 — context: transient plays its role in the serialization concept.</p>
<p>We can use transient keyword in serialization context.</p>
<p>(Full serialization programs come later — ~6–7 hours of that topic. Here: basic idea only.)</p>
<h3>37:26 — Serialization / deserialization (basic idea)</h3>
<p>Serialization: save / send the state of an object to a file (or across network).</p>
<p>Deserialization: reverse — read state of an object from the file.</p>
<p>Example sketch:</p>
<ul><li>Have an Account object.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Want to save it to abc.ser (extension is convention; any extension is fine).</li></ul>
<ul><li>Saving object state to file = serialization.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Reading it back = deserialization.</li></ul>
<p>File lives on hard disk → permanent storage.</p>
<h3>39:05 — Why `transient`? Security while serializing</h3>
<p>While saving object data to a file, some fields may be sensitive.</p>
<p>Board example:</p>
<ul><li>username = &quot;Durga&quot;</li></ul>
<ul><li>password = &quot;Anushka&quot;</li></ul>
<p>Saving username + password permanently to a file is not recommended — security risk / misuse.</p>
<p>Classroom analogy:</p>
<ul><li>Sharing mail ID: often OK.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Sharing password: almost nobody will share — dangerous if username and password travel together.</li></ul>
<p>So: username you may save; password you should not save at serialization time.</p>
<p>Rule: If you don’t want to save the value of a particular variable (to meet a security constraint), declare that variable `transient`.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Account {
    String username = &quot;Durga&quot;;
    transient String password = &quot;Anushka&quot;;
}</pre>
<h3>41:26 — What happens if a variable is `transient`?</h3>
<p>At serialization time:</p>
<ul><li>JVM ignores the original value of the transient variable.</li></ul>
<ul><li>JVM saves the default value to the file instead.</li></ul>
<p>For String password → default is `null`.</p>
<p>Conceptual after serialize / deserialize:</p>
<pre class='code'>import java.io.*;

class Account implements Serializable {
    String username = &quot;Durga&quot;;
    transient String password = &quot;Anushka&quot;;
}

class SerializeDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Account a1 = new Account();

        ObjectOutputStream oos =
            new ObjectOutputStream(new FileOutputStream(&quot;abc.ser&quot;));
        oos.writeObject(a1);
        oos.close();

        ObjectInputStream ois =
            new ObjectInputStream(new FileInputStream(&quot;abc.ser&quot;));
        Account a2 = (Account) ois.readObject();
        ois.close();

        System.out.println(a2.username + &quot;---&quot; + a2.password);
        // prints Durga---null
        // username kept; transient password → default null
    }
}</pre>
<p>Board meaning:</p>
<p>`transient` means not to serialize (not to save that field’s original value).</p>
<p>At serialization:</p>
<p>JVM ignores original value of transient variables and saves default value to the file.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Account {
    String username = &quot;Durga&quot;;
    // &quot;not to serialize&quot; this sensitive field
    transient String password = &quot;Anushka&quot;;
}</pre>
<p>Diagram idea (board): object → abc.ser with username kept, password replaced by default.</p>
<h3>47:38 — Transient wrap-up; next: `volatile`</h3>
<p>Serialization details deferred. Next modifier: `volatile` (Whisper: “wallet tile”).</p>
<h3>48:00 — Where can we apply `volatile`?</h3>
<p>Point 1:</p>
<ul><li>volatile is a modifier.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Applicable only for variables.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Cannot apply anywhere else (not methods, not classes).</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    // CE: modifier volatile not allowed here
    volatile void m1() {
    }
}
class Test {
    // valid
    volatile int x;
}</pre>
<p>(He notes: more on multi-threading later; here conceptual introduction.)</p>
<h3>49:11 — Meaning of “volatile” (everyday + memory analogy)</h3>
<p>Everyday: volatile = keep on changing; no stable behavior.</p>
<p>Hardware analogy he uses:</p>
<ul><li>Volatile memory → RAM (data lost on shutdown).</li></ul>
<ul><li>Non-volatile → hard disk (data remains after restart).</li></ul>
<p>Idea: volatile things keep changing.</p>
<h3>50:32 — When do we need `volatile`? Multiple threads + one variable</h3>
<p>Suppose variable x is accessed by multiple threads (T1, T3, T4, …):</p>
<ul><li>One thread sets 20, another 30, another −10, …</li></ul>
<ul><li>Value keeps changing → risk of data inconsistency.</li></ul>
<p>To overcome (in his framing of this chapter): declare the variable `volatile`.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Shared {
    // value may keep changing across threads
    volatile int x;
}</pre>
<h3>51:37 — Kids-and-ball story → separate copy per thread</h3>
<p>Long analogy:</p>
<ul><li>Festival; kids share one ball → fights / crying.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Solution: buy separate balls for every kid → problem solved.</li></ul>
<p>Same idea for threads:</p>
<ul><li>Multiple threads accessing / mutating the same variable → inconsistency risk.</li></ul>
<ul><li>If variable is declared `volatile` (as he teaches here): JVM creates a separate local copy for every thread.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Modifications by one thread happen in its copy → no effect on remaining threads.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Data-inconsistency problem is “solved” in this story.</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>class Counter {
    volatile int x = 0;

    // each thread works with its own local copy (as per lecture framing)
    public void update(int value) {
        x = value;
    }
}</pre>
<p>Advantage of `volatile`: overcome data inconsistency problem.</p>
<p>Disadvantage:</p>
<ul><li>If 10,000 threads → 10,000 copies.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Creating and maintaining a separate copy per thread increases complexity.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Performance goes down.</li></ul>
<p>Therefore (his OCJP-classroom conclusion):</p>
<ul><li>volatile is almost deprecated / not recommended to use without specific need.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Causes serious performance problems.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Many Java programmers barely know it — “not required” in day-to-day coding as he frames it.</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>class Demo {
    // works, but he says: never recommended without specific requirement
    volatile int sharedFlag;
}</pre>
<h3>56:54 — Board conclusions for `volatile`</h3>
<p>If the value of a variable keeps on changing by multiple threads → chance of data inconsistency.</p>
<p>Solve by declaring variable `volatile`.</p>
<p>If declared volatile:</p>
<ol><li>For every thread, JVM creates a separate local copy.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Every modification by a thread happens in that copy.</li></ol>
<ol><li>No effect on remaining threads.</li></ol>
<pre class='code'>class SharedData {
    volatile int x = 10;

    public void m1() {
        x = 20; // changes this thread&#x27;s local copy (lecture model)
    }
}</pre>
<h3>1:03:04 — Illegal combo: `final` + `volatile`</h3>
<p>Declaring volatile gives JVM big work: create and maintain a separate local copy per thread, because the value keeps changing.</p>
<p>But if the variable is `final`:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    final int x = 10;
}</pre>
<ul><li>final → value is fixed; never changes.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Nobody will change the value.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Then: what is the need of volatile / per-thread copies?</li></ul>
<p>Declaring final + volatile → compiler rejects: unnecessary / misuse of syntax.</p>
<p>`volatile final` is an illegal combination for variables.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    // CE: illegal combination of modifiers: final and volatile
    final volatile int x = 10;
}
class Test {
    // CE: illegal combination of modifiers: volatile and final
    volatile final int y = 20;
}</pre>
<p>Board logic:</p>
<p>Contradiction → illegal together.</p>
<h3>1:06:38 — All 12 modifiers covered — summary table begins</h3>
<p>He lists the 12 modifiers covered across the series:</p>
<ol><li>public</li></ol>
<ol><li>default (no modifier)</li></ol>
<ol><li>protected</li></ol>
<ol><li>private</li></ol>
<ol><li>final</li></ol>
<ol><li>abstract</li></ol>
<ol><li>static</li></ol>
<ol><li>synchronized</li></ol>
<ol><li>strictfp</li></ol>
<ol><li>native</li></ol>
<ol><li>transient</li></ol>
<ol><li>volatile</li></ol>
<p>Big table: for each modifier, is it applicable to…</p>
<p>| Column ideas on board |</p>
<p>| --- |</p>
<p>| Outer classes |</p>
<p>| Inner classes |</p>
<p>| Methods |</p>
<p>| Variables |</p>
<p>| Blocks |</p>
<p>| Outer interfaces |</p>
<p>| Inner interfaces |</p>
<p>| Outer enums |</p>
<p>| Inner enums |</p>
<p>| Constructors |</p>
<p>Gaps / blank cells = not applicable.</p>
<h3>1:12:03 — Filling the table (revision highlights)</h3>
<h4>Outer classes</h4>
<p>Applicable: public, default, final, abstract, strictfp.</p>
<h4>Inner classes</h4>
<p>Those five, plus extra: private, protected, static.</p>
<pre class='code'>public final class OuterAllowed {
}

class OuterDefault {
}

abstract class OuterAbstract {
}

strictfp class OuterStrict {
}
class Outer {
    private class InnerPrivate {
    }

    protected class InnerProtected {
    }

    static class NestedStatic {
    }
}</pre>
<h4>Methods</h4>
<p>Almost everything except last two:</p>
<ul><li>Allowed: access modifiers, final, abstract, static, synchronized, native, strictfp</li></ul>
<ul><li>Not for methods: transient, volatile</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>class MethodMods {
    public final synchronized void m1() {
    }

    public static strictfp void m2() {
    }

    public native void m3();

    // CE: modifier transient not allowed here
    // transient void bad1() { }

    // CE: modifier volatile not allowed here
    // volatile void bad2() { }
}</pre>
<h4>Variables</h4>
<ul><li>Allowed: access modifiers, final, static, transient, volatile</li></ul>
<ul><li>Not for variables: abstract, synchronized, native, strictfp</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>class VarMods {
    public static final int MAX = 100;
    private transient String secret;
    volatile int flag;

    // CE: abstract not allowed on variables
    // abstract int x;

    // CE: synchronized not allowed on variables
    // synchronized int y;

    // CE: native not allowed on variables
    // native int z;

    // CE: strictfp not allowed on variables
    // strictfp double d;
}</pre>
<h4>Blocks</h4>
<p>Only two meaningful cases:</p>
<ul><li>static block</li></ul>
<ul><li>synchronized block</li></ul>
<p>All other modifiers not applicable on blocks.</p>
<pre class='code'>class BlockMods {
    static {
        System.out.println(&quot;static block&quot;);
    }

    void m() {
        synchronized (this) {
            System.out.println(&quot;synchronized block&quot;);
        }
    }
}</pre>
<h4>Interfaces (outer)</h4>
<p>Similar to classes, but:</p>
<ul><li>Every interface is abstract by default → `final` not possible on interface.</li></ul>
<ul><li>public, default, abstract (implicit), strictfp OK.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Modifier applicable for class but not interface: `final`.</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>public interface I1 {
}

// CE: modifier final not allowed here / illegal for interface
// final interface I2 { }

strictfp interface I3 {
}</pre>
<h4>Inner interfaces</h4>
<p>Like outer interface rules (no `final`), plus extras like inner classes: private, protected, static.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Outer {
    private interface InnerI {
    }

    protected static interface NestedI {
    }
}</pre>
<h4>Enums</h4>
<p>Enum is like a class, but:</p>
<ul><li>Every enum is always final implicitly (discussed fully in enum chapter).</li></ul>
<ul><li>Even though always final, you cannot declare `final` explicitly.</li></ul>
<ul><li>You cannot declare enum abstract (in the simple rule he states here).</li></ul>
<ul><li>Modifiers applicable for classes but not enums: `final` and `abstract`.</li></ul>
<ul><li>public, default, strictfp OK for outer enum.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Inner enum: same idea + private / protected / static allowed like inner types.</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>public enum Color {
    RED, GREEN, BLUE
}

// CE: cannot declare enum final explicitly (already final implicitly)
// final enum Bad1 { A }

// CE: cannot declare enum abstract (as per this summary rule)
// abstract enum Bad2 { A }
class Outer {
    private enum InnerEnum {
        A, B
    }

    static enum NestedEnum {
        X, Y
    }
}</pre>
<h4>Constructors</h4>
<p>Only access modifiers:</p>
<ul><li>public, private, protected, default</li></ul>
<p>Any other modifier on a constructor → compile-time error.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public Test() {
    }

    private Test(int x) {
    }

    protected Test(String s) {
    }

    Test(double d) { // default access
    }

    // CE: modifier final not allowed here
    // final Test(boolean b) { }

    // CE: modifier static not allowed here
    // static Test(char c) { }

    // CE: modifier synchronized not allowed here
    // synchronized Test(long n) { }
}</pre>
<h3>1:19:34 — Table complete; “gap = not applicable”</h3>
<p>If a cell is empty in the summary table → that modifier is not applicable for that member kind. Cross-mark or leave blank — your choice.</p>
<h3>1:22:36 — Extra conclusions under the table (exam favorites)</h3>
<h4>1) Only applicable modifier for **local variables**</h4>
<p>Already covered earlier: only `final`.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public void m1() {
        final int x = 10; // only applicable modifier for local variable

        // CE: public not allowed for local variable
        // public int y = 20;

        // CE: static not allowed for local variable
        // static int z = 30;

        // CE: transient not allowed for local variable
        // transient int t = 40;
    }
}</pre>
<h4>2) Only applicable modifiers for **constructors**</h4>
<p>public, private, protected, default — nothing else.</p>
<pre class='code'>class C {
    public C() {
    }

    private C(int a) {
    }

    protected C(String s) {
    }

    C(byte b) {
    }
}</pre>
<h4>3) Modifiers applicable **only for methods** (nowhere else)</h4>
<p>From the table: `native` is the clear method-only tick (all other columns cross).</p>
<pre class='code'>class OnlyMethod {
    public native void m1(); // only for methods

    // cannot apply native on class / variable / etc.
}</pre>
<p>(synchronized also appears mainly on methods/blocks — but his spoken punchline here is `native` as the method-specific modifier.)</p>
<h4>4) Modifiers applicable **only for variables**</h4>
<p>`transient` and `volatile`.</p>
<pre class='code'>class OnlyVars {
    transient int a;
    volatile int b;
}</pre>
<h4>5) Applicable for **classes but not interfaces**</h4>
<p>`final`.</p>
<p>final class OkClass {
}

// CE: final interface not allowed
// final interface BadInterface { }</p>
<h4>6) Applicable for **classes but not enums**</h4>
<p>`final` and `abstract`.</p>
<p>abstract class OkAbstractClass {
}

final class OkFinalClass {
}

// not for enum (explicit final / abstract) — see enum rules above</p>
<p>Exam may ask these “which modifier where?” questions — keep the complete picture.</p>
<h3>1:27:14 — Closing / next topic</h3>
<p>Summary of all modifiers concept ends here.</p>
<p>Next topic: interfaces — “most valuable concept” for interviews / OCJP track.</p>
<h2>Quick revision card</h2>
<pre class='code'>// native — method only, no body
class N {
    static {
        System.loadLibrary(&quot;lib&quot;);
    }

    public native void m1();
}
// transient — skip original value at serialization
class A implements java.io.Serializable {
    String user = &quot;Durga&quot;;
    transient String pwd = &quot;secret&quot;;
}
// volatile — variable only; not with final
class V {
    volatile int x;
    // final volatile int y = 1; // CE
}
// constructors — only access modifiers
class T {
    public T() {
    }

    private T(int x) {
    }
}
// local variable — only final
class L {
    void m() {
        final int x = 10;
    }
}</pre>
